A minimal request like this:
```
  CURL *curl = curl_easy_init();
  curl_easy_impersonate(curl, "chrome136", 1);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, "https://www.google.com");
  curl_easy_perform(curl);
```
produces garbled output. 
This doest not happen for some other sites (eg https://tls.peet.ws/api/all) - or if the `curl_easy_impersonate` line is commented out.

This issue has been reproduced on versions 1.2.2 and 1.2.0. Version 0.6.0 works correctly.
I might investigate further to determine in which version the error/regression was introduced.

When the TLS hello packet is not long enough, BoringSSL will add a padding extension:

```cpp
    // Add padding to workaround bugs in F5 terminators. See RFC 7685.
    //
    // NB: because this code works out the length of all existing extensions
    // it MUST always appear last (save for any PSK extension).
    if (header_len > 0xff && header_len < 0x200) {
      // If our calculations already included a padding extension, remove that
      // factor because we're about to change its length.
      if (padding_len != 0) {
        header_len -= 4 + padding_len;
      }
      padding_len = 0x200 - header_len;
      // Extensions take at least four bytes to encode. Always include at least
      // one byte of data if including the extension. WebSphere Application
      // Server 7.0 is intolerant to the last extension being zero-length. See
      // https://crbug.com/363583.
      if (padding_len >= 4 + 1) {
        padding_len -= 4;
      } else {
        padding_len = 1;
      }
    }

    if (padding_len != 0 &&
        !add_padding_extension(&extensions, TLSEXT_TYPE_padding, padding_len)) {
      return false;
    }
  }
```

Some JA3 implementation(https://tls.browserleaks.com/json) will ignore this extension, since "it's padding", but some(https://tls.peet.ws/api/all) will consider this part of the JA3 string. Should we add an option to toggle this behavior? Was this used or ignored in real world?

